Frankly, I don't understand what I'm being accused of here.  In fact,
we might be talking about different things, though using very similar
language, and failing to communicate at all.

In hopes that we might be able to clear the air, let me try to put
forward a few ideas:

There are quite a number of different ways of looking at evolution.
For instance:

    The genes that are good at maintaining their existence tend to
    remain in existence.

Now, that's not too surprising.  It's almost tautologous.
Turtle-genes are a pretty good example of these genes -- they maintain
their existence unchanged for long periods of time.

Now, consider a similar but different statement:

    The genes that are good at makeing many individuals tend to be
    present in many individuals.

This is also almost tautologous.  But it gives different information,
about different genes.  Plankton-genes are a good example of these
genes.

Here's a third statement:

    Genes that are good at exterminating their alleles tend to be
    homologous at their loci.

That's also almost tautologous.  And it gives yet different
information.  I can't quote particular species as examples here.
Hemoglobin genes are a good example.  (Although there is a genus of
fishes without hemoglobin.)

The point is that "sociobiological" reasoning can be looked at from
many different points of view, and each view gives us different
information.  Each view is just a different model or filter used to
look at the huge complexity of nature.  And each view tends to give
information in a way that is compatible with the approach that the
view started with -- views that examine cooperation tend to show where
and how cooperation manifests itself; views that examine competition
tend to show where and how competition manifests itself.

Now, if we pick the particular view that asks about competition, we
get the obvious answer: Genes that are good at squeezing out genes
will squeeze other genes out.  If we examine history, the bulk of all
genes that have ever existed have no present descendents.  (The
squeezers are pretty good at squeezing.)  Of course, "squeezing" is a
very broad concept here, but as all but a very few nutrients are in
short supply in any well-developed ecosystem, there is intense
competition in all aspects of life.

Not that this means that the number of surviving species is very small
-- that's clearly false.  But species will probably succumb at a
rather high rate, so the number of surviving species will be small
compared to the number of species that have ever existed.  (Compare
capitalism:  The number of companies in existence is small compared to
the number of companies that have gone out of existence.)

Dale

