> Nadir (the highest point on the celestial sphere --upward and outward)

Wrong.  That's "zenith"; "nadir" is the opposite *lowest* point.

Both words, like many star names, are Arabic.  The history behind this
is that the classical and Alexandrian astronomical traditions were forgotten
in Europe, but not in the Arab world.

The early flowering of Islamic civilization, between the 7th and 10th centuries
CE, supported a great deal of leading-edge science, especially in astronomy 
but also in mathematics and chemistry (which is why our words "alcohol" and
"algebra" are also Arabic, including the fossilized "al-" Arabic definite
article).

Why didn't they follow up?  Fundamentally, their economic base was fragile;
there's not much good cropland in North Africa, especially, and the Sahara has
been steadily eating into that as it expanded throughout historical times.
Soil salinization due to over-irrigation did a similar job over much of the
Middle East.  And goats.  Goats are hell on ground cover; unlike cows, they
evolved in tough conditions and are *very* efficient eaters.

Economic shrinkage encouraged, and then was exacerbated by, internecine
warfare.  The beginning of the end was the breakup of the Ummaiyad caliphate
into contending successor states.  After that it was all downhill, with
science essentially proscribed by periodic fundamentalist revivals.

Islam-the-religion has a pattern of chugging more or less peacefully along
for a century at a time getting amiably fat and sloppy, until some mad prophet
rides out of the desert and galvanizes everybody into being good austere
Muslims again.  There's no distinction between secular and religious law in
Muslim tradition, and political legitimacy has (until the 20th century)
always been tied to religious authority and descent from the Prophet; thus,
the arrival of religious reformers tends to be accompanied by considerable
bloodshed and changes in the power structure.

The most recent movement of this type, the Wahhabi of the late 19th century,
created modern Saudi Arabia.  The Ayatollah Khomeini in Iran was something of
a mutant; as a Persian and a Shiite, his ideology is unlikely to have a
long-term draw for the 90% Sunni majority in Islam.  The thing to watch for
--- and fear --- would be a Khomeini-wannabe from the Sunni tradition.  Keep
an eye on the Sahel and North Africa; the poverty there breeds mad prophets
like flies.

And your scary thought for the month; the next one could pop up in Kazakhstan,
hard by a bunch of ex-Soviet nukes...
