Date: Thu, 8 Apr 93 23:00:15 PDT
From: hal@alumni.cco.caltech.edu (Hal Finney)
Subject: INTELL: Mental skills
X-Reposting-Policy: Redistribute freely

One book I read on the subject of intelligence testing emphasized the use
of the statistical technique called factor analysis to separate out and
identify various mental skills that go into what we generally consider
intelligence.  In this view there is no single overall mental trait that
represents total intelligence.  Instead, there are a wide variety of
useful mental abilities; people who do well in many areas would be those
we generally think of as intelligent.

I don't remember all of the areas discussed, but this book (which was a
scholarly study) identified twenty or so.  Memory was one general area,
but people's memories are quite varied.  There are seperate memory skills
for memorizing digits, words, and pictures, along with a generalized
memory skill.  Another area is "production" - how many answers a person
can produce to an open-ended question, possibly within a limited time.
"How many uses can you come up with for a shovel?"  "Name different ways
to wash a window."  Again, sub-skills are identifiable for various
problem domains, including production of words and numbers meeting some
criteria, as distinct from the more task-oriented questions above.

Other areas include "closed" logic, such as the standard syllogisms, or
puzzle questions like the ones which give facts about the ordering of
some set (Mary sits next to the baker's wife) and get you to deduce facts
from the givens.

The interesting thing is that although there are some correllations
among connected skills (like the memory skills), there is not a large
correspondence between skills in one area and skills in others.  Just
because a person has a good memory and is creative in coming up with
ideas, for example, he may be no more likely than average to have good
logical-deduction skills.

I find that this way of looking at intelligence does correspond pretty
well with people whom I have known, many of whom were quite "intelligent"
but in very different ways.  It helps to understand some of the confusing
mixes of ability and blind spots we run into so often.  On this view,
our estimate of a person's intelligence, or our measurement of it on an
IQ scale, is simply a projection of a multi-dimensional vector onto a
single line.  You lose a lot of useful information doing that.

