Date: Fri, 09 Apr 93 06:01:51 GMT
From: price@price.demon.co.uk (Michael Clive Price)
Subject: FTL: traversable wormholes

Robin Hanson expresses is his concerns that holes might lead to
centralising forces and terrorism:

> Imagine a wormhole or set of wormholes which approach allowing some
> closed causal loop to exist as some complex path through them.  [...]
> all these holes suddenly collapse, then global coordination would be
> required to manage a network of holes across spacetime. [...] The
> topology of holes would have to be designed not only to allow a
> minimum average number of jumps per trip, but also so that a minimum
> number of travel holes are destroyed by a rogue hole.

Both these requirements are met by flooding a volume with holes, or more
precisely with high capacity holes.  The capacity of a hole is the
throughput a hole can sustain before it collapses.  If rogue holes
invade an area to redefine local empire time it becomes a battle of
might.  A perimeter of really big holes would secure a volume against
invasion.  Any smaller holes, connected to a different empire time,
would burn out as they approached.  Provided the defender holes were big
enough they could absorb the energy flux that such encounters create as
a temporary hiccup.

Flooding a volume with holes would be a good way of staking a claim. 
It secures a volume, in addition to putting down markers.  I don't see
the need for central control.  Sounds like stability would grow with the
size of the civilisation.  And the surest way of staking an inviolable
claim to a volume (at the civilisation level) would be to allow free-
market colonisation/homesteading.

Those civilisations, or sub volumes, that tried to maintain an
centralised iron grip on hole proliferation would make themselves
vulnerable to exterior chronological invasion.

> A sign of two civilizations meeting would then be massive destruction
> of holes along a growing frontier area.

Fireworks at the "Neutral Zone" :-)  The front of an expanding sphere
of civilisation would be holes travelling at close to light speed,
leaving a trail of smaller holes behind, like buoys on a fishing net
tossed over the side of moving trawler, that grow in size as colonists
move in, bringing more holes with them.  The only holes destroyed as two
civilisation meet will be the in-flight holes.  The holes trailed behind
would be left in operation.  This would define a natural merging of
empire time up to the Neutral Zone.

> [...] And with massive hole destruction, arranging meetings for
> negotiations might take a while in empire time.  Here's a
> good Schelling point problem - where should hole ends meet to merge
> the nets?

Where/when better than the neutral zone itself.  Say, there is *great*
potential here for SF!

Each civilisation *could* try to impose it own empire time over already
alien-colonised areas - but it would be very expensive.  More
importantly they would have no incentive to.  Connecting up with other
civilisations would be the only way of breaking out of the future light
cone defined by local empire time.  By cooperating with other
civilisations we meet with we can travel to regions of space time that
would otherwise be forever barred by relativity.  Trying to invade them
with *our* wormholes would reduce the space-time available to us.

> For peacetime trading, a tremendous
> "time wind" (wind of change?) would allow much faster velocities
> (relative to empire time) for moving mass out from the centre,
> compared with moving mass toward the centre.

I don't follow the "time wind" business, could you explain?.  Sounds
wonderful, though.  The advantages I can see for the centre are inertia,
history and the ability to throw rocks on colonists "lower" down, as you
described.

> Robin Hanson

