Date: Fri, 9 Apr 93 00:56:26 -0700
From: mcpherso@lumina.ucsd.edu (John McPherson)
Subject: FAQ: Immortality, what has been achieved towards it?

   So far I've received three responses to my "priorities" question.
Two people considered personal immortality to be their top priority,
as it is mine, hence my next question:  "What has already been
achieved towards personal immortality?"

   I know a (very) little about some of the related areas:

a. Life Extension -- Dr. Roy Walford's reduced calorie diet, Durk
   and Sandy's work, the Life Extension Foundation (vs. the FDA!),
   and Life Extension International's catalog of supplements.

   This work is of value, but by itself can't produce personal
   immortality (as far as I know).  Perhaps it can give us that
   extra 10-20 years when immortality becomes accessible (to _us_)
   ... if that's how long it takes.  Perhaps I'm missing something
   though, is it possible that a future program of nutrients and
   drugs could actually produce perpetual rejuvenation?

b. Cryonic Preservation -- Alcor's program, and some research by 
   other companies.

   This currently seems to be our best shot at "surviving" until
   such time as immortality becomes accessible to us, but, of course,
   it doesn't give us immortality directly.  Unless I'm wrong, the
   current status of the field is that heads/bodies can be frozen,
   but ice crystal damage is too great to reanimate people successfully.

c. Nanotech Restoration -- I'm aware of Eric Drexler's work, but I
   haven't yet read his books.  The idea of nanotech seems great,
   but how long will we have to wait before we can apply it to 
   achieving personal immortality?  Can anyone give a brief 
   description of what has actually been achieved so far in this 
   field?  In general, what are the current hot research topics?

d. Uploading -- I'm aware of Moravec's work, but haven't yet read 
   his books.  The idea of uploading my consciousness into a
   super-advanced computer/robot (which I'm guessing is what Moravec
   means) is a little beyond my conceptual reach right now ... 
   I can understand, in principle, the idea of replacing neurons
   one-at-a-time with superior functionally-'identical' neuron-like
   units until there are no actual neurons left, but the idea of my
   actually _doing_ that gives me the willies.  I suppose I should
   give a fair reading to _Mind Children_ before I come to a firm
   conclusion.


   What have I missed?  Are there other major technologies that
I've inadvertently left out?  Are there any major developments and
breakthroughs in the above fields that I missed?


ps -- Since no one mentioned it, I take it that there is _not_ 
      an Extropian FAQ available ... is that true?

